From: Andrew Morton <akpm@linux-foundation.org>
Subject: mm/rmap: avoid reusing batched TLB flush generations
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 17:49:27 -0800

mm->tlb_flush_batched resets to 1 when its 15-bit pending count grows
large.  A concurrent flush can read 1, flush, then see 1 again after
the reset and mark newer unmaps as flushed.  A subsequent caller can
skip a needed TLB flush and leave a stale writable translation.

Use the existing atomic_t as two bits instead of counting generations.
PENDING records a deferred unmap.  A caller atomically changes PENDING
to FLUSHING before flushing.  Any other caller seeing FLUSHING flushes
for itself rather than treating the first flush as complete.  An unmap
that arrives during the flush sets PENDING again.  The first caller
clears only FLUSHING after its flush, preserving that new work.
There are no generation values to wrap or for a delayed flusher to
write back, and mm_struct does not grow.

The unmapper holds the PTE lock through the clear and PENDING update.
smp_mb__before_atomic() orders the clear before atomic_or(), which has
no memory ordering of its own.  A successful cmpxchg takes ownership
before flush_tlb_mm().  The flush completes before atomic_andnot()
clears FLUSHING.  flush_tlb_mm() provides the required ordering of TLB
invalidation completion; the final atomic operation only needs to
preserve PENDING if an unmap races with that clear.

All callers that check the state hold the relevant PTE lock.  Once a
checker acquires that lock, its zero-state read cannot miss an unmap
which published PENDING before releasing it.  A zero read, including a
failed cmpxchg returning zero, can also follow a completed flush by
another owner.  An unmap under another PTE lock cannot clear the entry
this checker is about to change.

Initialize the state for a new mm: dup_mm() copies the parent's
mm_struct before mm_init(), so the child must not inherit an in-progress
flush with no owner.

The owner executes a cmpxchg before flush_tlb_mm() and an
atomic_andnot() afterward, rather than one post-flush cmpxchg in
the counter code.  This adds an atomic read-modify-write operation to
an uncontended pending-flush call.  The unmap path also replaces a
compiler barrier with smp_mb__before_atomic(), which can cost more on
weakly ordered CPUs.

Concurrent flushes can cause additional full-mm flushes.  If an unmap
sets PENDING while the owner flushes, a second checker can flush and
cover that unmap, but cannot mark it complete.  The owner clears only
FLUSHING, leaving PENDING for a third checker to flush again.  With
counters, the second checker could record the newer generation as
flushed.  These costs have not been measured.

Fixes: 5ee2fa2f0636 ("mm/rmap: fix potential batched TLB flush race")
Signed-off-by: Andrew Morton <akpm@linux-foundation.org>
Reported-by: Kirk Swidowski <swidowski@google.com>
Cc: <stable@vger.kernel.org>

 include/linux/mm_inline.h |    3 +
 mm/rmap.c                 |   65 ++++++++++++++----------------------
 2 files changed, 29 insertions(+), 39 deletions(-)

--- a/include/linux/mm_inline.h~mm-rmap-avoid-reusing-batched-tlb-flush-generations
+++ a/include/linux/mm_inline.h
@@ -574,6 +574,9 @@ void pfnmap_track_ctx_release(struct kre
 static inline void init_tlb_flush_pending(struct mm_struct *mm)
 {
 	atomic_set(&mm->tlb_flush_pending, 0);
+#ifdef CONFIG_ARCH_WANT_BATCHED_UNMAP_TLB_FLUSH
+	atomic_set(&mm->tlb_flush_batched, 0);
+#endif
 }
 
 static inline void inc_tlb_flush_pending(struct mm_struct *mm)
--- a/mm/rmap.c~mm-rmap-avoid-reusing-batched-tlb-flush-generations
+++ a/mm/rmap.c
@@ -732,20 +732,18 @@ void try_to_unmap_flush_dirty(void)
 }
 
 /*
- * Bits 0-14 of mm->tlb_flush_batched record pending generations.
- * Bits 16-30 of mm->tlb_flush_batched bit record flushed generations.
+ * PENDING marks an unmap needing a flush. FLUSHING marks the caller
+ * flushing earlier unmaps. New unmaps can set PENDING during that flush;
+ * only the caller that claimed FLUSHING clears it after its flush.
+ * Other callers seeing FLUSHING must flush for themselves.
  */
-#define TLB_FLUSH_BATCH_FLUSHED_SHIFT	16
-#define TLB_FLUSH_BATCH_PENDING_MASK			\
-	((1 << (TLB_FLUSH_BATCH_FLUSHED_SHIFT - 1)) - 1)
-#define TLB_FLUSH_BATCH_PENDING_LARGE			\
-	(TLB_FLUSH_BATCH_PENDING_MASK / 2)
+#define TLB_FLUSH_BATCH_PENDING	1
+#define TLB_FLUSH_BATCH_FLUSHING	2
 
 static void set_tlb_ubc_flush_pending(struct mm_struct *mm, pte_t pteval,
 		unsigned long start, unsigned long end)
 {
 	struct tlbflush_unmap_batch *tlb_ubc = &current->tlb_ubc;
-	int batch;
 	bool writable = pte_dirty(pteval);
 
 	if (!pte_accessible(mm, pteval))
@@ -754,24 +752,9 @@ static void set_tlb_ubc_flush_pending(st
 	arch_tlbbatch_add_pending(&tlb_ubc->arch, mm, start, end);
 	tlb_ubc->flush_required = true;
 
-	/*
-	 * Ensure compiler does not re-order the setting of tlb_flush_batched
-	 * before the PTE is cleared.
-	 */
-	barrier();
-	batch = atomic_read(&mm->tlb_flush_batched);
-retry:
-	if ((batch & TLB_FLUSH_BATCH_PENDING_MASK) > TLB_FLUSH_BATCH_PENDING_LARGE) {
-		/*
-		 * Prevent `pending' from catching up with `flushed' because of
-		 * overflow.  Reset `pending' and `flushed' to be 1 and 0 if
-		 * `pending' becomes large.
-		 */
-		if (!atomic_try_cmpxchg(&mm->tlb_flush_batched, &batch, 1))
-			goto retry;
-	} else {
-		atomic_inc(&mm->tlb_flush_batched);
-	}
+	/* Publish the PTE clear before recording the pending flush. */
+	smp_mb__before_atomic();
+	atomic_or(TLB_FLUSH_BATCH_PENDING, &mm->tlb_flush_batched);
 
 	/*
 	 * If the PTE was dirty then it's best to assume it's writable. The
@@ -811,19 +794,23 @@ static bool should_defer_flush(struct mm
  */
 void flush_tlb_batched_pending(struct mm_struct *mm)
 {
-	int batch = atomic_read(&mm->tlb_flush_batched);
-	int pending = batch & TLB_FLUSH_BATCH_PENDING_MASK;
-	int flushed = batch >> TLB_FLUSH_BATCH_FLUSHED_SHIFT;
-
-	if (pending != flushed) {
-		flush_tlb_mm(mm);
-		/*
-		 * If the new TLB flushing is pending during flushing, leave
-		 * mm->tlb_flush_batched as is, to avoid losing flushing.
-		 */
-		atomic_cmpxchg(&mm->tlb_flush_batched, batch,
-			       pending | (pending << TLB_FLUSH_BATCH_FLUSHED_SHIFT));
-	}
+	int state = atomic_read(&mm->tlb_flush_batched);
+
+	if (!state)
+		return;
+
+	/* Only one caller clears FLUSHING; others flush for themselves. */
+	if (state == TLB_FLUSH_BATCH_PENDING)
+		state = atomic_cmpxchg(&mm->tlb_flush_batched,
+				       TLB_FLUSH_BATCH_PENDING,
+				       TLB_FLUSH_BATCH_FLUSHING);
+	if (!state)
+		return;
+
+	flush_tlb_mm(mm);
+	if (state == TLB_FLUSH_BATCH_PENDING)
+		atomic_andnot(TLB_FLUSH_BATCH_FLUSHING,
+			      &mm->tlb_flush_batched);
 }
 #else
 static void set_tlb_ubc_flush_pending(struct mm_struct *mm, pte_t pteval,
_
